q1. Limit occurences in sorted array:

📝 Problem in Simple Words
We are given a sorted array and a limit k.
We need to ensure that no element appears more than k times.
Return the new array after limiting occurrences.

🚀 My Initial Approach (Brute Force)
Traverse the whole array.

Count occurrences of each element.

Stop when count > k, skip extra duplicates.

Complexity: O(n).

✅ Correct intuition.
❌ Missed optimization: didn’t leverage the fact that the array is sorted (duplicates are consecutive).

⚡ Optimal Approach
Since the array is sorted, duplicates form blocks.
We can use two/three pointers (start, low, mid) to process each block:

cpp
vector<int> limitOccurrences(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
    vector<int> res;
    int n = nums.size();
    int start = 0;

    while (start < n) {
        int low = start;
        int mid = start;

        // move mid until value changes
        while (mid < n && nums[mid] == nums[start]) {
            mid++;
        }

        int freq = mid - low;          // block size
        int allowed = min(freq, k);    // limit occurrences

        for (int t = 0; t < allowed; t++) {
            res.push_back(nums[start]);
        }

        start = mid; // jump to next distinct element
    }
    return res;
}
Time Complexity: O(n)

Space Complexity: O(n) for result

🔎 Where I Missed / Gaps to Improve
Initially thought about binary search (lower_bound, upper_bound) but that’s better for count queries, not for reconstructing the limited array.

Needed to highlight that sorted property = consecutive duplicates = block compression trick.

Interview gap: must explain why brute force wastes time and why sorted property changes the game.

🧠 Memory Hook
Think: “Sorted → Blocks → Limit”

Sorted array → duplicates are consecutive.

Count block size → mid - low.

Limit with min(freq, k).

Quick formula: allowed = min(freq, k)

🎯 Interview Tip
When explaining:

Start with brute force O(n).

Then refine: “Since the array is sorted, duplicates form blocks. Using start/low/mid pointers, I can count each block and limit occurrences in one pass.”

Bonus: Mention this is a classic block compression trick in arrays

