q3. minimum operation to sort a permutation 


  Problem in Simple Words
We are given a permutation of [0..n-1].
Allowed operations:

Rotate left by one (move first element to end).

Reverse the entire array.

We need the minimum number of operations to make the array sorted ascending. If impossible → return -1.

🚀 My Initial Approach
Try brute force: apply rotation repeatedly, check if sorted.

If not sorted, try reverse + rotations.

Return count if sorted.

✅ Correct brute force intuition.
❌ Gap: brute force wastes time.
👉 Needed to realize only two valid targets exist: sorted ascending or reverse sorted.

⚡ Optimal Approach
Check ascending possibility:

Find index of 0.

Rotate until 0 is at front.

Verify sorted.

Count rotations.

Check reverse possibility:

Find index of n-1.

Rotate until n-1 is at front.

Verify reverse sorted.

Count rotations + 1 (for reverse).

Answer = min(valid rotations). If none valid → -1.

💻 Contest‑Style C++ Code
cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int minOperations(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();

        auto checkSorted = [&](vector<int>& arr) {
            for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) 
                if (arr[i] < arr[i-1]) return false;
            return true;
        };

        auto checkReverseSorted = [&](vector<int>& arr) {
            for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) 
                if (arr[i] > arr[i-1]) return false;
            return true;
        };

        // Try rotations for sorted
        int idx0 = find(nums.begin(), nums.end(), 0) - nums.begin();
        vector<int> rotatedSorted(n);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) 
            rotatedSorted[i] = nums[(i+idx0)%n];
        if (checkSorted(rotatedSorted)) return idx0;

        // Try rotations for reverse sorted
        int idxN = find(nums.begin(), nums.end(), n-1) - nums.begin();
        vector<int> rotatedRev(n);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) 
            rotatedRev[i] = nums[(i+idxN)%n];
        if (checkReverseSorted(rotatedRev)) return idxN + 1; // +1 for reverse

        return -1;
    }
};
🔎 Where I Missed / Gaps to Improve
Initially thought brute force rotations were necessary.

Missed that only two target forms matter: sorted ascending or reverse sorted.

Interview gap: must explain why brute force is wasteful and why permutation property simplifies the problem.

🧠 Memory Hook
Think: “Rotation aligns, Reverse flips”

Rotation → bring 0 or n-1 to front.

Reverse → flips order.

Formula: Ops = rotations (+1 if reverse needed).

🎯 Interview Tip
When explaining:

Start with brute force idea.

Then refine: “Since it’s a permutation, only two valid targets exist: sorted ascending or reverse sorted. So I only need to check rotations aligning 0 or n-1.”

Bonus: Mention complexity: O(n) (linear check after rotation).

📝 English Grammar Note
Original:
“after reverse and rotation we will check if sorted if true then return the no of count.”

Improved:
“After performing reverse and rotation, we check if the array is sorted. If it is, we return the number of operations.”

👉 Fixes:

Add “performing” for clarity.

Use “we check if the array is sorted” instead of “we will check if sorted”.

Replace “no of count” with “number of operations”.

  

